Declarative vs Imperative Infrastructure

✨ Hello DevOps Learners!
Continuing my GitOps learning journey after completing Ansible, today I’m exploring an important concept in infrastructure management: Declarative vs Imperative Infrastructure. 🚀
Understanding this difference is key to how GitOps works with modern DevOps tools.

🔹 Imperative Infrastructure
 In the imperative approach, you tell the system exactly how to perform each step.
Commands are executed manually or through scripts
You control the sequence of actions
Example: running multiple commands to create and configure resources
This approach can become complex and harder to maintain as systems grow.

🔹 Declarative Infrastructure
 In the declarative approach, you simply define the desired end state of your system.
Configuration is written in YAML or configuration files
Tools automatically make the system match the defined state
Example: defining Kubernetes resources using YAML manifests

🔹 YAML-Based Deployments
 In Kubernetes and GitOps workflows, applications and infrastructure are defined using YAML configuration files.
 These files describe:
Pods
Deployments
Services
Configurations
The system then ensures the cluster matches what is defined in the YAML files.

🔹 Infrastructure as Code Connection
 Declarative infrastructure is closely related to Infrastructure as Code (IaC).
Examples:
Terraform defines infrastructure declaratively
Kubernetes YAML defines application deployment state
Together, they help create automated, reproducible, and version-controlled infrastructure.

💡 Key Idea:
 GitOps relies on the declarative model, where the desired system state is stored in Git and automatically enforced.
